package cosmonic:cron@0.1.0;

/// Being called when a schedule comes due.
///
/// This is the trigger. A workload exports it, and whatever implements
/// `scheduler` calls it, so the workload holds no timer and does not have to
/// exist between firings: an instance starts when a schedule is due and can
/// retire when the call returns.
///
/// ## Delivery semantics
///
/// - **At most one firing of a schedule is handled at a time.** If a schedule
///   comes due while its previous firing is still being handled, that firing
///   is skipped and counted in the next delivered firing's `missed`. Different
///   schedules are independent and their firings may be handled concurrently.
/// - **A firing is a signal, not a durable message.** One that cannot be
///   delivered, because the workload is stopped or the implementer was not
///   running, is skipped and counted, not stored and replayed.
/// - **A firing may be delivered more than once**, when the handler asks for
///   that with `retry`. `name` and `scheduled-at` together identify it, and
///   `attempt` counts the deliveries.
@since(version = 0.1.0)
interface handler {
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    use types.{firing};

    /// Why a firing was not handled.
    ///
    /// The cases are what the caller cannot work out for itself: whether
    /// delivering the same firing again could help.
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    variant handler-error {
        /// Deliver this firing again. It may succeed later: a dependency was
        /// unavailable, a lock was held, a quota was spent.
        ///
        /// The caller redelivers after a delay, a bounded number of times. For
        /// a repeating schedule it also gives up once the next firing is due,
        /// so a retry never overlaps or delays the schedule itself.
        retry(option<string>),
        /// Do not deliver this firing again. A repeating schedule carries on
        /// and its next firing is delivered as usual.
        failed(option<string>),
        /// A failure not captured above, treated as `failed`.
        ///
        /// This is also the case a caller sees when the call could not be
        /// completed at all (the handler trapped, ran past a deadline, or its
        /// workload stopped mid-call) and the runtime in between had to
        /// produce the error on the handler's behalf.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// Handle one firing.
    ///
    /// Returning `ok` means the firing was handled. Returning an error never
    /// cancels a repeating schedule; a handler that wants its schedule to stop
    /// calls `scheduler.unregister`. A schedule that fires once is spent
    /// whatever its handler returns, once any retries are over.
    ///
    /// The detail carried by an error is for a log, not for matching on.
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    handle: async func(firing: firing) -> result<_, handler-error>;
}
